        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.639,
            0.049,
            0.846,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Constructions and Tilings"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.088,
            0.846,
            0.126
          ],
          "content": "Covering a region using a set of shapes, without gaps or overlaps, is called **tiling**."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.127,
            0.681,
            0.146
          ],
          "content": "Consider a rectangu<u>lar grid made of unit square</u>s —"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.367,
            0.149,
            0.644,
            0.297
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_21_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.314,
            0.718,
            0.334
          ],
          "content": "We call this a $4 \\times 6$ grid, since it has 4 rows and 6 columns."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.343,
            0.722,
            0.389
          ],
          "content": "? Can a $4 \\times 6$ grid be tiled using multiple copies of $2 \\times 1$ tiles? We are allowed to rotate a $2 \\times 1$ tile and use it."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.816,
            0.328,
            0.904,
            0.383
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_21_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.343,
            0.399,
            0.401,
            0.478
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.33,
                0.483,
                0.415,
                0.497
              ],
              "content": "Vertical tile"
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_21_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.561,
            0.434,
            0.671,
            0.48
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.556,
                0.484,
                0.666,
                0.499
              ],
              "content": "Horizontal tile"
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_21_image_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.512,
            0.316,
            0.531
          ],
          "content": "Here is one way."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.315,
            0.532,
            0.672,
            0.706
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_21_image_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.719,
            0.584,
            0.74
          ],
          "content": "Obviously, this is not the only tiling possible."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.752,
            0.546,
            0.776
          ],
          "content": "Can a $4 \\times 7$ grid be tiled using $2 \\times 1$ tiles?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.788,
            0.394,
            0.812
          ],
          "content": "What about a $5 \\times 7$ grid?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.815,
            0.847,
            0.855
          ],
          "content": "To see that there is no way to tile a $5 \\times 7$ grid using $2 \\times 1$ tiles, observe that this grid has 35 unit squares. Each tile covers exactly 2 unit squares."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
